  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Frost/freeze conditions are probable Monday night \nand Tuesday night \n\nThe coldest air of the early Autumn season will push southeast \ninto Central PA early in the upcoming week with frost/freeze \nconditions expected Monday night and Tuesday night over at least\nthe NW half of the CWA, with min temps around the freezing mark\nacross the Northern Tier counties of PA.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2: Little to no rainfall forecast through Saturday, \nOctober 10th\n\nIncreasingly moist low-level southeasterly flow will bring \nthickening/low clouds and a few periods of very light \nrain/drizzle to areas along and east of the Allegheny Front and \nsouth of Interstate 80 today into this evening. \n\nTotal rainfall is expected to be just a few to several hundredths\nof an inch in most places that see rain today, with an outside \nshot at up to 0.10 of an inch across portions of the Lower Susq\nValley, NE along the I-81 corridor (across the higher terrain \nof Schuylkill County where llvl upslope flow will be maximized).\nThis will be the only precip for the next 7 days.\n\nA secondary cold front followed by colder WNW flow could bring \na couple of showers downwind of Lake Erie tonight into early \nMonday as lake/850 mb delta Ts will be 18-19 deg C. \n\nA subtle subsidence inversion based at 7-9 KFT AGL Monday and\nmore notable inversion based at 5-6 kft agl Monday night, \nrelatively short fetch over Lake Erie and the lack of very cold \nair aloft with cloud top temps of 0 to -5C will lead Lake- \nInduced CAPE of up to 500 J/KG Monday and 300-400 J/KG Monday \nnight/Tue Morning. This will bring some thin/meandering bands \nof SHRA that will extend into our NW zones at times. \n\nOtherwise, we continue to have high confidence in a prolonged \ndry stretch through next week as a large area of Canadian high \npressure dominates the pattern.",
